 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101090 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101090 #edb/ref 1045 "Minimizing a Function Using the Graph of its Derivative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101090 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101090 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101090 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101090 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5b7df4cb-5457-52fa-980a-0eb4f115707f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101169 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101169 #edb/ref 1069 "q-144824" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101169 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101169 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a4/a4a238887e789979e276421850330c5b36a82c485312c06e831c0df96266b85b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5]$ given that $f(-1)=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101169 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9dcfbb8-b458-5675-b01f-0a851451c52c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101169 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =1 \\\\ f(5) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,7\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101662 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101662 #edb/ref 1061 1580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101662 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "004e3ad6-6e61-5d62-b4ae-2312bfc01180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101662 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101663 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101663 #edb/ref 1061 1581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101663 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "862b5691-1ffd-5f5a-8f20-30b383a2b4a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101663 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101664 #edb/ref 1061 1585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101664 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18a6128e-8cf7-576b-91e8-2271bc331d8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101664 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101815 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101815 #edb/ref 1061 38636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101815 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "92b50514-ef00-5094-9587-bbcdf5bd94d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101815 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102026 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to use the graph of a function's derivative to minimize the function. Let's understand how this process works by studying a specific example.\n\nThe graph of $y = f'(x),$ the *derivative* of $f(x),$ is shown in the picture below. \n\n![](images/2e/2ee1721d439ee0234b0f8a99ed0e4a56c63041e36919b00978267cf361a5322c.png)\n\nThe areas of the shaded regions are indicated on the diagram. Additionally, it is known that $f(-2)=5.$ Our goal is find the *minimum* value of $f(x)$ on the interval $x\\in [-2,2].$\n\nTo find the minimum value of $f(x),$ we use the candidates test.\n\nAccording to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. Therefore, we proceed as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ so there are no non-stationary critical points. Additionally, we see that $f'(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are \n\n$$\nx=-2,\\quad x=1,\\quad x=2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\n- The value of $f(x)$ at the left endpoint is given:\n\n$$\nf(-2) = 5\n$$\n\n- To compute $f(1),$ we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus. Since the areas of the rectangle and middle triangle in our picture equal ${\\color{red}{2}}$ and ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac12}},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5-2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we *subtracted* these areas since both lie *under* the $x$-axis.\n\n- To compute $f(2),$ we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus once more. Using the fact that the area of the right triangle equals ${\\color{blue}{\\dfrac12}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5-2-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\left\\lbrace 5, \\dfrac52, 3 \\right\\rbrace = \\dfrac52.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{min}}=\\dfrac52,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102026 #edb/ref 1065 822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102026 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a6d3aebd-5589-5160-b57f-ed0eb4f47691" #edb/