1079 #uuid "d6416470-1d7a-5a75-a4b7-d19f68263b06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512857 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186512135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512857 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512857 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512857 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186512135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512857 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f3887f5c-297a-596e-b84b-b76944fcf812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512879 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512879 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512879 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512879 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/19/192a69596e851fa35b02bbfd6bda06f01814d0d1c05b5698a4179d00a4bb14d2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512879 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ef4f411-6c67-5c13-837f-d11ff722cfe0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512879 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(-8) \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,-10\\}=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-10,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512919 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512919 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512919 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512919 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/91/91441cecdbe477e4cf0115c1f7c4cf413fa36786690ff7e025ac2e62c711f66e.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ if $h(-2)=6.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512919 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a823921c-a56b-531a-8f8b-ef8538df5571" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512919 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in [-2,4],$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/19/1996a5ab1052a63a9513ed57a6a74ea82ad5994b62288cb04e4df02dac7589b6.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-2) & =6 \\\\ h(0) & =h(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6+2 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(3) & =h(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8+3 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ h(4) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11+(-1) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $h$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{6,8,11,10\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{min}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512922 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512922 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512922 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512922 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0c/0c23b2d07d656695feda6ae6e92d192661e30f2738b6af8342813b44f2885667.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $6,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512922 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aeb0f117-1019-5572-b78f-39043f8c3ded" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512922 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,6,0\\}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=0,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512947 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512947 #edb/ref 1069 "q-19083" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512947 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512947 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/42/429cba100e1cebd4a2048226f3c9d252fdec171b33679ab8ff6ba21014386aa4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de36a313-5e05-5888-a50c-ca20f5e00cf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512947 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/d2/d20759e0c246f1fad5e0a36e495302c6b8de0e23a5f8ed25bc291147750a1342.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1)+4.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,3,7.5\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512950 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/98/98290071482fa2a8082932304fbb0d0cc0db54c47324479da7979079e9542551.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6$ and $9$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,3]$ given that $f(-4)=-1.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2f10b5f-3a80-55aa-94de-01383831523e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512950 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6-9 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,5,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512953 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186512857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512953 #edb/ref 1069 "q-342944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512953 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512953 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ab/ab8e0cbaf83156609db79ec08202c8f77acfebd03663dfe273e4d75f9db03afb.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ if $f(0)=4.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512953 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f237f691-54ad-5d16-bd80-dc2031f08456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186512953 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2,4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,2,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ea/ea837252a62313eae506ef3d101c0b70121f6380694a49c2f54e1a19702949a5.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3) \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3)+0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,5,2,2.5\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
